- Model moved to device: [/workspace/repo/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers.ipynb:532-537] shows:
    import torch
    device = torch.device('cuda') if torch.cuda.is_available() else torch.device('cpu')
    torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark = True
    model.to(device)

- Training loop moves batches to the same device: [/workspace/repo/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers.ipynb:566-571] shows:
    for batch in train_dataloader:
      batch = {k: v.to(device) for k, v in batch.items()}
      outputs = model(**batch)

- Evaluation loop incorrectly moves batches to CPU while the model remains on the (GPU) device: [/workspace/repo/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers.ipynb:578-586] shows:
    for batch in train_dataloader:
      batch = {k: v.to('cpu') for k, v in batch.items()}
      with torch.inference_mode():
        outputs = model(**batch)

  This causes implicit device transfers when calling model(**batch) if model is on CUDA, violating the test requirement.

- Prediction loop (test set) correctly moves batches to device; this inconsistency between evaluation (CPU) and prediction (device) further demonstrates inconsistent device handling: [/workspace/repo/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers/disaster-tweets-classification-using-transformers.ipynb:639-639] shows:
    for batch in test_dataloader:
      batch = {k: v.to(device) for k, v in batch.items()}

- No dtype policy found: repository contains no use of autocast/amp or explicit dtype casting (searched for bf16/bfloat16/float16/autocast/half), indicating default fp32 is used everywhere (i.e., no explicit dtype policy applied).

Because of the explicit CPU move in the evaluation loop while the model is placed on device, the test fails.

